STM32G4: turn on the ADC's hardware oversampler

Betaflight's G4 ADC driver feeds single conversions to the voltage filter.
On the T-Motor Pacer G4 Alpha HD the battery-sense pin (PA0) is noisy enough
(raw SD about 2 V on a steady 22.9 V supply, samples from 0 to 40 V) that the
3 s display filter never settles within the 0.1 V that voltageIsStable()
needs, so the cell count stays at 0: the OSD shows 0.00 average cell and the
low-battery warnings have no threshold.

Averaging 256 conversions per result (shifted right 8, still 12-bit) took the
raw SD to 0.13 V and the filtered SD to 0.016 V. The stability test then
passed 100% of the time, and 6S locked 3.6 s after power came up. At
640.5-cycle sampling and fADC = 42 MHz each result takes about 4 ms per
channel, well inside the 50 Hz battery task.

Applies to Betaflight 2026.6.2 (master is identical for this file as of
2026-11-11). From the root of the Betaflight source:

    patch -p1 < stm32g4-adc-oversampling.patch

--- a/src/platform/STM32/adc_stm32g4xx.c
+++ b/src/platform/STM32/adc_stm32g4xx.c
@@ -222,7 +222,14 @@
     hadc->Init.ExternalTrigConvEdge = ADC_EXTERNALTRIGCONVEDGE_NONE;
     hadc->Init.DMAContinuousRequests = ENABLE;
     hadc->Init.Overrun = ADC_OVR_DATA_OVERWRITTEN;
-    hadc->Init.OversamplingMode = DISABLE;
+    // Average 256 conversions in hardware for each result, shifted back to 12 bits. At 640.5-cycle
+    // sampling and fADC = 42 MHz that is about 4 ms per channel, so a full scan still lands well
+    // inside one 50 Hz voltage-task period, and spikes on a noisy divider average out.
+    hadc->Init.OversamplingMode = ENABLE;
+    hadc->Init.Oversampling.Ratio = ADC_OVERSAMPLING_RATIO_256;
+    hadc->Init.Oversampling.RightBitShift = ADC_RIGHTBITSHIFT_8;
+    hadc->Init.Oversampling.TriggeredMode = ADC_TRIGGEREDMODE_SINGLE_TRIGGER;
+    hadc->Init.Oversampling.OversamplingStopReset = ADC_REGOVERSAMPLING_CONTINUED_MODE;
 
     if (HAL_ADC_Init(hadc) != HAL_OK) {
         handleError();
